INT. ROADSIDE MOTEL LAUNDRY ROOM - NIGHT

A cinderblock room with six humming dryers and a vending machine that sells nothing. A single bulb, bare, buzzes. A mop bucket stands in a pool of gray water. The door to the lot is propped open with a brick.

A CLERK (30s, name tag crooked, plastic key ring on his wrist) folds towels at a steel table. RHONDA (40s, bathrobe over street clothes, a baby monitor in her hand) sits on a plastic chair watching a dryer spin. TRUDY (60s, hair curlers, cardigan buttoned wrong) stares into a washer as it fills. LAMAR (20s, hoodie, headphones, a garbage bag of clothes at his feet) leans against the wall.

The clerk looks up when a dryer buzzes. Looks at the others. Looks back at the towels.

RHONDA
You're not going to fold mine, are you?

CLERK
No, ma'am.

RHONDA
Good.

TRUDY
There's a stain on my sleeve that won't come out.

CLERK
Which one?

TRUDY
The red one.

CLERK
I don't think that's coming out.

TRUDY
That's what everyone says.

Lamar pulls off his headphones. Everyone is looking at the washer. The water inside has turned pink.

LAMAR
Is that normal?

CLERK
It happens.

LAMAR
What happens?

CLERK
Colors bleed.

Rhonda holds the baby monitor to her ear. A faint hiss of static. She turns the knob. The hiss grows, and under it, a slow wet breathing.

RHONDA
Trudy, did you bring a baby?

TRUDY
Of course not.

The Clerk sets down the towel. His eyes drift to the open door, the dark lot, the single car with its engine ticking.

CLERK
(quietly)
Dale. My name is Dale.

LAMAR
What?

DALE
You asked me my name, earlier. I wasn't ready to say.

The overhead bulb flickers. Rhonda lowers the monitor slowly. The breathing now comes from the dryer, the one still turning, the one with the small round window.

DALE (CONT'D)
Nobody open that.

Trudy opens it.
